But note: if the target is the child itself, `child._reorient` returns a copy of the child with copied children. Then I add the old self as its child. Good.

If the target is deeper, `child._reorient` returns a reoriented subtree (a new node) that already has, as a child, the copy of `child` minus the path. Then I append the old self as a child of that reoriented subtree. Good.

